/ (Root): The top-level starting directory of the entire system.

    /etc: Contains system-wide and application configuration files.

    /var: Stores variable data that changes over time, such as system logs (/var/log).

    /proc: A virtual filesystem generated by the kernel in RAM, providing real-time hardware and process metrics (e.g., /proc/cpuinfo, /proc/meminfo).

    /home: Personal storage directories for regular users.

    /tmp: Holds temporary files, usually cleared upon system reboot.

    /usr: Contains user utilities, commands, and shared applications (e.g., /usr/bin).


    df -h: Displays overall filesystem disk space usage in human-readable units.

    du -sh <path>: Summarizes the cumulative disk space used by a specific directory.

    lsblk: Lists information about all available block storage devices.

    cat /etc/os-release: Shows detailed distribution information about the OS.


    which <command>: Returns the full path of an executable binary (e.g., /usr/bin/git).

    whereis <command>: Finds the binary, source code, and manual page locations.

    find <directory> -name "<pattern>": Performs a real-time, deep search through the filesystem.

    locate <filename>: Queries a pre-indexed database for near-instant results.
